# 3. User Roles and Permissions

Implement three principal roles with strictly separated permissions.

## A. Student

Students can:
- Sign in using their university-issued email and password.
- Access only their own profile, applications, documents, notifications, and generated decisions.
- Browse application types currently available to them.
- Create, save, edit, and submit applications according to the workflow rules.
- Upload required documents.
- View fees, fines, payment instructions, and payment verification status.
- Track the entire application timeline.
- See the current responsible department or staff role where disclosure is permitted.
- View estimated processing time and applicable deadlines.
- Receive notifications when an application advances, is returned, is rejected, or requires action.
- Download completed applications and official documents.
- Review approval signatures and stamps attached to issued documents.
- Request clarification or respond to a returned application.
- Withdraw a draft or request cancellation where policy permits.

Students must never access another student's records by changing an application ID, URL, query parameter, or API request.

## B. Staff

Each staff member receives an account created or provisioned by an authorized administrator.

Staff can:
- Sign in securely using their assigned university email and password.
- View a personalized staff dashboard.
- View only applications assigned to them or their authorized department.
- See pending verification tasks, signature requests, overdue tasks, completed tasks, and upcoming deadlines.
- Open an application and inspect permitted fields and uploaded documents.
- Verify or reject documents with a mandatory explanation when rejection is appropriate.
- Approve or reject a stage when their permissions allow it.
- Request corrections from a student.
- Apply an authorized electronic signature or official stamp.
- Forward an application to the next configured workflow stage.
- View the full history of actions taken on an application.
- Record an internal processing note when authorized.
- See their task turnaround time and overdue assignments.

Staff must not create administrator accounts, modify workflow definitions, assign themselves unrestricted permissions, or access unrelated departments' applications.

An action such as "Verify" must actually execute the corresponding server-side transition, persist the result, generate an audit event, notify relevant users, and update the application timeline.

## C. Administrator

The administrator manages the entire university workspace and has the highest application-level privileges.

Administrator capabilities include:
- Overview dashboard and analytics.
- Student and staff account management.
- Staff invitation, credential provisioning, activation, suspension, and password reset.
- Department and designation management.
- Application type and template management.
- Workflow creation, editing, publishing, versioning, and retirement.
- Workflow stage configuration and ordering.
- Staff, department, and role assignment for each stage.
- Document requirement configuration.
- Fee and fine rules.
- Deadline and escalation policy configuration.
- Electronic signature and stamp asset management.
- Application monitoring and controlled reassignment.
- Notification templates and delivery settings.
- Audit log access.
- Reports, export, and operational analytics.
- University branding and organization settings.

The administrator must be able to configure the system without modifying application source code.

Administrator privileges must still be enforced server-side. Avoid unrestricted access based only on hidden UI controls.

